From 56ff81cbea53f36cddf2c3fe7d82d6d6d81c20b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: unxed <ivan.sorokin.tech@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:24:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] redox: posix_spawn: take CLONE_LOCK like fork does

pthread_create on Redox builds the new thread from a set of handles ("new-thread",
"current-addrspace", "current-filetable", "start") that are held in the creating
process's file table. The initial instruction and stack pointers, and the file table, are
applied by the kernel when the "current-addrspace" / "current-filetable" handle is
CLOSED, i.e. when the last reference to it is dropped. fork() therefore holds
CLONE_LOCK for writing (pthread_create holds it for reading) so that its file-table
snapshot cannot contain those in-flight handles. posix_spawn takes the same kind of
snapshot for the child but did not take the lock.

If a thread is being created in the parent while another thread spawns, the child's
snapshot holds a second reference to the "current-addrspace" handle; the parent's close
then does not apply the registers, and the new thread is started with all registers zero.
It faults at once ("Page fault: 0000000000000000 US | ID", RIP = 0, RSP = 0); the
kernel kills that thread but the rest of the process keeps running, typically with its
main thread blocked in pthread_join forever: the program hangs.

Hold CLONE_LOCK for writing in Sys::spawn, as fork does.
---
 src/platform/redox/mod.rs | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/platform/redox/mod.rs b/src/platform/redox/mod.rs
index 7fe9ea9..55a98de 100644
--- a/src/platform/redox/mod.rs
+++ b/src/platform/redox/mod.rs
@@ -1201,6 +1201,14 @@ impl Pal for Sys {
         envp: Option<NulTerminated<*mut c_char>>,
     ) -> Result<pid_t> {
         use crate::header::spawn::Flags;
+        // Like fork(): keep pthread_create out while the child gets a snapshot of this process's
+        // file table. A thread being created holds handles that only take effect when the
+        // last reference to them is closed (the "current-addrspace" handle sets the new thread's
+        // instruction and stack pointers when it is closed); a reference in the child's snapshot
+        // keeps that from happening, and the new thread then starts with all registers zero:
+        // "Page fault: 0000000000000000 US | ID" at RIP 0. The thread's process is left with
+        // its other threads blocked forever (pthread_join never returns).
+        let _guard = CLONE_LOCK.write();
         let child = redox_rt::proc::new_child_process(&redox_rt::proc::ForkArgs::Managed)?;
         let mut cwd = path::clone_cwd().unwrap_or_default();
         let mut cwd_fd = FdGuard::open(cwd.as_str(), syscall::O_STAT)?.to_upper()?;
